WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:02.760
Film Courage: What is a dangling cause?

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:03.790
Paul Joseph Gulino, Associate Professor at
Chapman University Dodge College of Film and

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:08.420
Media Arts: A dangling cause is one of the
tools that keeps the audience anticipating.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:16.070
And it comes from I believe French formalism
narratology (the narrative theory) and simply

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:21.610
stated we respond (we talk about this in the
book) we respond to cause and effect.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:23.290
We look for cause and effect.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:26.260
We are geared toward understanding the world
that way.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:34.399
It’s easy for us to follow stories, narrative
events when there’s a cause and effect that’s

00:00:34.399 --> 00:00:35.399
evident.

00:00:35.399 --> 00:00:41.120
Dangling cause is a cause that you don’t
get the effect right away but you anticipate.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.620
So boy likes girl, boy kisses girl.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:47.629
Cause and effect.

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:52.729
Boy likes girl, boy says “I’m going to
kiss her before the weekend is over.”

00:00:52.729 --> 00:00:53.729
Dangling cause.

00:00:53.729 --> 00:00:59.579
We’re going to pick up that effect later
but it dangles over in the audience’s consciousness.

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:06.230
So it’s one of the tools in the screenwriting
(the sequence approach) that we talk about

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:11.480
that helps keep the audience wondering what’s
going to happen next?

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:16.710
I mentioned deadline that telegraphing sort
of what is coming literally.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:22.040
There’s an appointment at 5 o’clock today,
we are going to meet at Jerry’s Juiceateria

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:26.100
tell the audience that when you cut there
you don’t have to explain why we’re there,

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.360
you’ve been told.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:38.460
The deadline like THE HURT LOCKER or AMERICAN
BEAUTY we know that when he’s close to being

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.060
dead the movie is going to be over.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:44.490
There is dramatic irony where we are engaged
because where we are engaged because we’re

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:49.280
going to see what happens when the truth comes
out and then there’s dramatic tension when

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:54.980
we’re wondering how would the person will
get what they want or not and will they escape

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.690
what they want or not.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:02:05.030
And I think if there’s anything in my approach
what we are trying to do here that I emphasize

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:07.750
with the students is there’s always talk
about rules.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:08.750
Should I follow the rules?

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:09.750
Should I break the rules?

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:15.920
Should I do this or that and to me the problem
with rules or understanding rules or even

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.340
if you want to call them guidelines is that
if you follow the rules congratulations.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.909
You’re one down, you’re following, you’re
following!

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:25.840
You are not leading.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.719
You’re following and having followed them
the rules don’t applaud and they don’t

00:02:31.719 --> 00:02:32.719
pay you anything.

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:35.359
A better question to ask is what’s the effect?

00:02:35.359 --> 00:02:39.950
What’s the effect of my choice in the story
on my audience?

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:42.499
Because now I’m one up, I’m in charge
of what is happening.

00:02:42.499 --> 00:02:48.049
I’m in charge of how I’m affecting my
audience and the tools that are in these books

00:02:48.049 --> 00:02:49.459
that’s the idea.

00:02:49.459 --> 00:02:52.629
What are the choices that you didn’t know
you had that you have that you can use to

00:02:52.629 --> 00:02:57.829
affect the audience and audiences do applaud
and they pay you.

00:02:57.829 --> 00:03:02.420
So I like my students to be in the power position
where they’re figuring out what they wants

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.950
to do rather than whether they can follow
something.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:09.549
Film Courage: So true or false if we focus
on the audience everything will fall into

00:03:09.549 --> 00:03:10.640
place with our screenplay?

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.940
Professor Gulino: If we focus on the audience
everything will fall into place?

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.469
True or false, no nuance?

00:03:18.469 --> 00:03:25.739
We focus on the audience we have a better
shot of succeeding at connecting with an audience,

00:03:25.739 --> 00:03:27.409
that’s the closest I can come.

00:03:27.409 --> 00:03:32.500
Film Courage: So focusing on the audience
keeping structure in mind, sequences, things

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:33.500
like that?

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:39.200
Professor Gulino: Yes and it gives you that
freedom if you know what they’re anticipating

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:44.620
and you can play games.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:45.620
How do you break a rule?

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:50.040
Well Alfred Hitchcock (I don’t want to give
it away but) Alfred Hitchcock exploited the

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:54.692
fact that in a Hollywood movie there’s a
main character and the main character is the

00:03:54.692 --> 00:03:57.239
star and the star lives through the movie.

00:03:57.239 --> 00:03:59.779
Maybe they die in the end but they live through
the movie.

00:03:59.779 --> 00:04:05.579
Knowing that’s what the audience is expecting
what happens if you do them off 40 minutes

00:04:05.579 --> 00:04:06.579
in?

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:09.099
Well that’s going to have a huge effect.

00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:13.750
Well you can do other things to make up for
the loss if you understand what the function

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:19.380
of a character is then you can do something
else to compensate for that and keep the audience

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.090
interested even though the character is gone.

00:04:21.090 --> 00:04:26.700
I’ll leave you with one joke and I know
we’ve got to end but it’s like this if

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:32.500
you were to write a book on knock-knock jokes
what would you do?

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:35.810
You would look at all the knock-knock jokes,
you say it’s very simple, these are the

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:37.750
rules.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:38.970
You say knock-knock.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:40.630
The other person says “Who is there?”

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:42.410
You give a partial answer.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.420
They say “Partial answer who?”

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:48.440
And then you give them the punchline okay?

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:49.970
That’s the rules.

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:54.410
That’s my book and that’s how if you want
to be successful.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:55.760
Okay knock-knock.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:58.460
Film Courage: Who’s there?

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:03.330
Professor Gulino: Control freak…okay now
here is how you say “Control freak who?”

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:09.000
See I just broke the rules but what I was
doing was trying to elicit was a laugh.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.320
The whole point was the effect not the rules.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:18.230
It’s knowing what you think, exploiting
the fact that you have a schema, an understanding

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:21.530
of what the knock-knock joke is I can play
games.

